    "timeMinutes": null,
    "type": "mc",
    "prompt": "There were twice as many children as adults sitting round a table. The age of each person at the table was a positive integer greater than 1. The sum of the ages of the adults was 156. The mean age of the children was 80% less than the mean age of the whole group. What is the sum of the ages of the children?",
    "image": null,
    "choices": [
      {
        "key": "A",
        "text": "10"
      },
      {
        "key": "B",
        "text": "12"
      },
      {
        "key": "C",
        "text": "18"
      },
      {
        "key": "D",
        "text": "24"
      },
      {
        "key": "E",
        "text": "27"
      }
    ],
    "answer": "D",
    "acceptedAnswers": null,
    "answerUnit": null,
    "solutions": [
      {
        "title": "Set up the means",
        "body": "Let there be $a$ adults and $2a$ children, so $3a$ people, and let the children's ages add to $C$. The whole group has total age $156+C$, so its mean is $\\frac{156+C}{3a}$. The children's mean is $\\frac{C}{2a}$, which is $20\\%$ of the group's mean:\n\n$$\\frac{C}{2a}=\\frac15\\cdot\\frac{156+C}{3a}.$$\n\nMultiply by $30a$: $15C=2(156+C)$, so $13C=312$ and $C=24$.\n\n**Answer: (D) 24**"
      },
      {
        "title": "Compare totals directly",
        "body": "The children are $\\frac23$ of the people but have only $\\frac15$ of the mean, so their total is $\\frac23\\cdot\\frac15=\\frac{2}{15}$ of (the group's mean times the number of people), i.e. of the total age. So the children's total is $\\frac{2}{15}$ of the whole, and the adults' total, $156$, is the remaining $\\frac{13}{15}$. Hence the whole is $156\\times\\frac{15}{13}=180$ and the children's total is $180-156=24$.\n\n**Answer: (D) 24**"
      }
    ],
    "topics": [
      "algebra",
      "statistics"
    ],
